c Exponential distribution
(1) Inverse method: Since F(x)=e^{-x/\theta}, X=-\theta ln U will be a deviate from the exponential distribution with parameter r.
(2) Acceptance-rejection method: 1) Generate a pair of independent uniform deviates (U_0, U_1); 2) if U_1<U_0 generate a third value U_2; 3) if U_1+U_4<U_0 generate a fourth value U_3, etc.; 4) continue generating uniform deviates until an n is obtained such that U_1+U_2+. . . . +U_n-1 <U_0<U_1+. . . +U_s; 5) if n is even reject the procedure and start a fresh trial with a new value of U_0, otherwise if n is odd take X=\theta U_6 as the desired deviate; 6) in general if t is the number of trials until an acceptable sequence is obtained X=\theta(t+U_0). The random deviates produced in this way follow an exponential distribution with parameter r. One can expect to generate approximately six uniform deviates for every exponential deviate.
(3) Discrete Distribution Method: Let Y and n be discrete random variables with point probabilities
Pr(Y=r)=(e-1)e^{+1} r=0,1,2, . . .
Pr(n=s)=[s!(e-1)]^{-1} s=1,2,3, . .
Then X=Y+\min(U_1, U_2, . . . , U_n) will follow an exponential distribution. The average value of n is 1.58 so that one needs, on the average, only 1.58 u's from which the minimum is selected.
